/* phase F und_r08: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["8a"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["8a"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"8a1.1","q":"5f sorted the fractions into two heaps around the hole. What does 8a1 add?","a":"everything in the lower heap is below everything in the upper heap, so the split is clean","w":["the lower heap has no greatest member","a fraction is found at the split","the upper heap holds the negative fractions"],"why":"No greatest member was 5f’s. The clean split comes from members-below-non-members, proved for any heap of this kind."},
 {"u":"8a2.1","q":"Why does the ladder keep only the lower heap of a splitting?","a":"the splitting is fixed by it: the upper heap is the fractions left over","w":["the upper heap is always empty","the lower heap is always the smaller set","the upper heap has no least member to name it by"],"why":"So a number can be one set of fractions, with four properties: a member, a fraction missed, closed downward, no greatest member."},
 {"u":"8a3.1","q":"A cut has four conditions. How do you make a cut?","a":"prove all four for your set of fractions","w":["prove any one of the four","name its greatest member","list its members"],"why":"A cut is used by unpacking: (i) and (ii) are somes, (iii) and (iv) are everys. It is made by proving all four."},
 {"u":"8a4.1","q":"What makes ℝ, the set of all cuts, a set?","a":"Power gives 𝒫(ℚ), and Separation sifts the cuts from it","w":["a new axiom laid down on rung 8","Replacement, on the fractions","the picture of a line with no gaps"],"why":"Nothing is assumed. “The line” is only the ladder’s name for ℝ with its order; no geometry is meant."},
 {"u":"8a5.1","q":"For a cut α, r ∈ α and s ∉ α. What follows, and by what kind of or?","a":"r < s, from the proved or of any-two-compare on ℚ","w":["r < s, from the either-way principle","s < r, since s is missed","r = s, by the swap axiom"],"why":"The cases s < r and s = r each put s in α, a clash. The or was proved on rung 5, so no P ∨ ¬P is cited."},
 {"u":"8a6.1","q":"For reals, α ≤ β means α ⊆ β. Why is order on the line “inherited, not invented”?","a":"reflexive, antisymmetric and transitive are three techniques of rung 2 about ⊆","w":["it is copied from the order on the fractions, member by member","it is laid down as an axiom","it is read off the picture of the line"],"why":"Self-part, double-inclusion and chain for part of. Whether any two reals compare is another question, 8b’s."},
 {"u":"8a7.1","q":"r* is the set of fractions below r. What do fraction-cut and fractions-keep-order say together?","a":"each r* is a real, and the cuts r* stand in the same order as the fractions r","w":["r* is the fraction r itself","r* has r as its greatest member","r* = s* for any two fractions"],"why":"So the fractions sit inside ℝ as a copy that keeps order: r < s exactly when r* < s*."},
 {"u":"8a8.1","q":"r ∈ α ⇔ r* < α. Which direction is classical?","a":"leftward: from r* < α to r ∈ α, which uses not-every","w":["rightward: from r ∈ α to r* < α","both directions","neither direction"],"why":"Rightward only chases a member. Leftward must get a member of α outside r* from “not every”, a rung 7 move."},
 {"u":"8a9.1","q":"What makes a real irrational?","a":"it is r* for no fraction r","w":["nobody has found its fraction yet","it has no members","it has a greatest member"],"why":"Hole’s-step gives a larger member still below the hole, which is cut (iv), and the hole’s cut is proved to be no fraction’s cut."},
 {"u":"8a10.1","q":"Why is {r ∈ ℚ : r ≤ ½} not a cut?","a":"½ is its greatest member, so (iv) fails","w":["it has no member, so (i) fails","it misses no fraction, so (ii) fails","it is not closed downward, so (iii) fails"],"why":"With it, ½ would have two cuts. And {r : r² < 2} fails (iii): 1 is in, −3 is below it and out."},
 {"u":"8a11.1","q":"Handed a real α, how do you find which fraction it is?","a":"look for the least fraction not in α; if there is none, α is irrational","w":["look for the greatest member of α","check whether α has a member","count the members of α"],"why":"α = r* exactly when r is the least fraction α misses. Checking a set for a cut, (iii) and (iv) need the work."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["8b"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["8b"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"8b1.1","q":"In any-two-compare on ℝ, the case ¬(α ⊆ β) hands one fraction r in α and not in β. Why is one enough?","a":"every member of β is below r, so every member of β is in α","w":["r is the greatest member of α","r is then in β after all","one fraction shows α = β"],"why":"Members-below-non-members puts each s ∈ β below r, and cut (iii) for α lets it in. So β ⊆ α."},
 {"u":"8b2.1","q":"Exactly one of α < β, α = β, β < α. Where does the proof use antisymmetry?","a":"in “at most one”: α < β and β < α would give α = β","w":["in “at least one”, to split on α = β","nowhere: either-way does all the work","to prove that α ≤ α"],"why":"Either-way and any-two-compare give at least one. Double-inclusion rules out two at once."},
 {"u":"8b3.1","q":"α < β. What does witness-for-less hand you?","a":"a fraction in β that α misses","w":["a fraction in α that β misses","a real halfway between them","the greatest member of α"],"why":"Less on the line always comes with a fraction caught between the two cuts. 8e uses it to put a fraction strictly between any two reals."},
 {"u":"8b4.1","q":"What does greater-is-the-merge say about max(α, β)?","a":"it is α ∪ β, and min(α, β) is α ∩ β","w":["it is α + β","it is α ∩ β","it is a new cut, neither α nor β"],"why":"If α ⊆ β the union is β and the intersection α; if not, the other way. So the union of two cuts is always one of the two."},
 {"u":"8b5.1","q":"α_P = {r : r < 0 ∨ (r < 1 ∧ P)}. What does comparing-gives-either-way show?","a":"if any two reals compare, then P ∨ ¬P for every statement P","w":["α_P is no real unless P is decided","the comparison can be proved without either-way","α_P = 0* whenever P is unknown"],"why":"α_P is a real with no either-way needed. Comparing it with a fixed cut would settle P, so the comparison is exactly as strong as the principle."},
 {"u":"8b6.1","q":"“α ⊈ β, so β ⊆ α.” Why does that hold for cuts and not for sets in general?","a":"it used members-below-non-members and cut (iii), which are facts about cuts","w":["it holds for any two sets of fractions","⊆ compares any two sets","it needs only that α and β have members"],"why":"{0} and {1} are parts of ℚ, and neither is a part of the other."},
 {"u":"8b7.1","q":"A comparison of two reals is built from a statement nobody has decided. What do you ask?","a":"what the comparison would tell you about the statement","w":["which of the two is listed first","whether the reals are fractions","how many members each has"],"why":"If it would settle the statement, the comparison came from either-way and has no address."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["8c"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["8c"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"8c1.1","q":"Why is the name sup S honest?","a":"a set has at most one least upper bound: two would each be at most the other","w":["every set of reals has a least upper bound","the least upper bound is always a member of S","an upper bound is always least"],"why":"If σ and σ′ are both least, σ ≤ σ′ and σ′ ≤ σ, so σ = σ′ by double-inclusion."},
 {"u":"8c2.1","q":"S is a set of reals with a member and an upper bound β. Which cut condition of ⋃S comes from β?","a":"(ii): a fraction β misses is missed by the union","w":["(i): the union has a member","(iii): the union is closed downward","(iv): the union has no greatest member"],"why":"Without a bound the union can swallow every fraction, as ⋃{n* : n ∈ ℕ} does, and then it is no cut."},
 {"u":"8c3.1","q":"On the ladder no-holes is proved. Why must a book that starts from the twelve techniques lay it down as an axiom?","a":"the fractions keep all twelve and have holes, so the twelve cannot prove it","w":["its proof needs the either-way principle","its proof needs Choice","it is false for some sets of reals"],"why":"Here sup S = ⋃S, with no either-way and no Choice cited. The proof leans on what a real is, which the twelve do not say."},
 {"u":"8c4.1","q":"α = sup{r* : r ∈ α}. What second reading does that give 8a’s slogan?","a":"a real is the set of the fractions below it, and also the least real above all of them","w":["a real is the greatest of its fractions","a real is a fraction with an endless bottom","a real is the union of every cut"],"why":"The union of the cuts r* for r ∈ α is α itself, by (iii) and (iv), and no-holes says the union is the sup."},
 {"u":"8c5.1","q":"H is the hole’s heap. What does {r* : r ∈ H} have on the line that H lacks among the fractions?","a":"a least upper bound","w":["an upper bound","a member","a greatest member"],"why":"H has fractional upper bounds such as 3/2, and under each one a smaller one. On the line the sup is the hole’s cut."},
 {"u":"8c6.1","q":"S = {(1 − 1/n)* : n ≠ 0} has sup 1*. Why is 1* not a member of S?","a":"it would be (1 − 1/n)* for some n, giving 1/n = 0","w":["1* is not a real","S has no upper bound","the union of S is all of ℚ"],"why":"Every fraction below 1 is below some 1 − 1/n, so the union is 1*. A sup need not belong to its set."},
 {"u":"8c7.1","q":"Why is there no sup ∅?","a":"every real is an upper bound of ∅, and there is no least real","w":["∅ has no upper bound","∅ is not a set","its sup is 0*"],"why":"No-holes needs a member. And inf S is not the common part of the members: the cuts (1/n)* share {r : r ≤ 0}, which has a greatest member."},
 {"u":"8c8.1","q":"You want σ = sup S without writing the union. What two things do you show?","a":"σ is an upper bound, and for any β < σ some member of S is above β","w":["σ is a member of S, and σ is positive","σ is a fraction, and S is a set of fractions","σ is above one member, and below one upper bound"],"why":"An upper bound that nothing smaller can replace is the least one."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["8d"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["8d"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"8d1.1","q":"Rung 5 built operations on representatives. Why is each operation on cuts a sift of ℚ instead?","a":"a cut is one set, with no representatives; the work is to check the four conditions","w":["cuts have too many representatives to choose from","sifts need no checking","the fractions have no operations to borrow"],"why":"Counting-passes-fractions comes first as a tool: for fractions w > 0 and c, some number n has c < n · w."},
 {"u":"8d2.1","q":"Why do turn-about and regroup for plus on ℝ come almost free?","a":"the members r + s obey them in ℚ, so the two sets have the same members","w":["they are laid down as axioms for the line","adding cuts is taking their union","they follow from the picture of the line"],"why":"α + β is {r + s : r ∈ α, s ∈ β}. A technique about members in disguise reduces to ℚ by same-members."},
 {"u":"8d3.1","q":"“−α is the negatives of the fractions α misses.” What goes wrong for α = r*?","a":"the set has a greatest member, −r, so it is no cut","w":["the set is empty","the set holds every fraction","the set is not closed downward"],"why":"The fix asks for room: r is in −α when some s above r has −s missed by α. That drops the one possible greatest member."},
 {"u":"8d4.1","q":"When is a real α positive?","a":"when 0* < α, which is exactly when 0 ∈ α","w":["when every member of α is positive","when α has a positive member and no negative one","when α misses 0"],"why":"By members-are-the-fractions-below. And α < β exactly when β − α is positive, so the order made from ⊆ is 5d’s order made from “positive”."},
 {"u":"8d5.1","q":"In the product of positive cuts, why is every fraction at most 0 let in?","a":"the positive products alone would miss the negatives, and (iii) would fail","w":["so that the product has a greatest member","so that (ii) fails","because negative fractions multiply to negatives"],"why":"A cut is closed downward. The reciprocal is built the same careful way as −α, through the fractions the cut misses."},
 {"u":"8d6.1","q":"αβ is defined by five cases on the signs. What makes the definition honest?","a":"three-way on ℝ: each real is exactly one of 0*, positive, negative, so exactly one case applies","w":["the five cases all give the same set","the cases are chosen by convenience","only positive reals are ever multiplied"],"why":"Signs-come-out then lets a minus on either factor come out in front, so every technique reduces to positive cuts."},
 {"u":"8d7.1","q":"Anything-times-zero and zero-product hold on the line with no new proof. Why?","a":"5e proved them from the twelve techniques alone, and the line has the twelve","w":["they are true by definition of a cut","they were proved for sets of fractions","they are laid down again as axioms"],"why":"Whatever was proved from the twelve alone holds wherever the twelve hold. That is 5e’s promise, collected."},
 {"u":"8d8.1","q":"The cuts of fractions add, negate, multiply, invert and order exactly as ℚ does. What naming line does that license?","a":"where a real is meant, a rational r names the real r*","w":["every real names a fraction","r* names the fraction r wherever a fraction is meant","ℚ and ℝ are the same set"],"why":"So 0 names 0* and 1 names 1*, as on 5b and 5c: a copy that keeps everything may lend its names."},
 {"u":"8d9.1","q":"Which kind of system are the fractions?","a":"a regular number system with holes","w":["a regular number system with no holes","not a regular number system","a system with no order"],"why":"Regular means the twelve techniques hold. No holes means every part with a member and an upper bound has a least upper bound; ℝ has that, ℚ does not."},
 {"u":"8d10.1","q":"Why is {rs : r ∈ α, s ∈ β} not the product of cuts?","a":"negatives multiply to large positives, so every fraction gets in and (ii) fails","w":["it has a greatest member","it is empty","it is the union α ∪ β"],"why":"For 1* and 1*, (−5)(−5) = 25 is in. And α + β is not α ∪ β: 1* ∪ 1* = 1*, while 1* + 1* = 2*."},
 {"u":"8d11.1","q":"A set of fractions is offered as an operation on cuts. What do you check first?","a":"(iv): a greatest member is the usual flaw, and the cure is to ask for room","w":["(i): it usually has no member","that it holds every fraction","that it is a union of two cuts"],"why":"Then ask what kind of technique you want: about members in disguise (reduce to ℚ), or about signs (pull the minuses out)."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["8e"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["8e"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"8e1.1","q":"Counting-passes-any-real is first proved through sup. What is the shape of that proof?","a":"suppose no number passes α; then the numbers have a sup σ, σ − 1 is no upper bound, and a number above it leads past σ: a clash","w":["list the numbers until one passes α","take the greatest number below α and add one","induction on α"],"why":"A proof by contradiction: no-holes hands σ, and the least bound cannot survive one more step of counting."},
 {"u":"8e2.1","q":"The direct column finds a fraction α misses and counts past it. What virtue does the sup proof keep?","a":"it works in any regular number system with no holes, however its members were built","w":["it hands you the number n","it is shorter","it avoids no-holes"],"why":"The direct column says more here, since it names n, and it is there because a cut carries a fraction it misses."},
 {"u":"8e3.1","q":"For every positive real α some 1/n is below α. What does that rule out on the line?","a":"a positive real below every 1/n: no infinitely small positive members","w":["a real above every number","a fraction between two reals","a least positive fraction"],"why":"If α were positive and below every 1/n, everyday-counting-passes would give an n with 1/n < α, a clash."},
 {"u":"8e4.1","q":"A fraction sits between any two reals. The first road is shorter; why give the second?","a":"it says what the fraction’s bottom can be: some multiple of 1/n lands in the gap","w":["the first road is not a proof","the second avoids counting","the second works only for fractions"],"why":"Steps of 1/n smaller than β − α cannot jump the gap. 8·cb5 uses this road for decimals."},
 {"u":"8e5.1","q":"“The numbers go on forever, so they pass α.” Why is that no proof?","a":"going on is not passing: the fractions 1 − 1/n go on forever and never pass 1","w":["the numbers do not go on forever","α might be a fraction","passing needs Choice"],"why":"And σ − 1 is not an upper bound when σ is the least one: anything below the least is not a bound."},
 {"u":"8e6.1","q":"A proof says some number exists. What do you ask of it?","a":"whether it says which: the cut road does, the sup road does not","w":["whether the number is a fraction","whether it used induction","whether the number is positive"],"why":"Wanting a number above a real, find a fraction the cut misses and count past it."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["8f"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["8f"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"8f1.1","q":"0 ≤ x < y and n ≥ 1. What does powers-keep-order give?","a":"xⁿ < yⁿ","w":["xⁿ > yⁿ","xⁿ = yⁿ","nothing, unless x and y are fractions"],"why":"By induction from n = 1. Square-never-negative says 0 ≤ x² for every real x. With three-way-order the order technique runs backwards too."},
 {"u":"8f2.1","q":"Root-of-two takes σ = sup{x : 0 ≤ x ∧ x² < 2}. Why does the column fail over the fractions?","a":"the line σ = sup S fails: among the fractions that set has no least upper bound","w":["fractions cannot be squared","everyday-counting-passes fails for fractions","three-way-order fails for fractions"],"why":"No-holes gave σ. Counting gave steps small enough not to overshoot, and the comparison of 8b said the third case stands once two are refuted."},
 {"u":"8f3.1","q":"√u names the one real x ≥ 0 with x² = u. Which two techniques make the name honest?","a":"square-root (there is one) and one-root (there is only one)","w":["square-never-negative and power-bound","no-holes and Choice","three-way-order and turn-about"],"why":"A name needs exactly one thing to name. And the hole’s cut of 8a is √2: 5f’s hole is filled."},
 {"u":"8f4.1","q":"What does power-bound do for roots of higher order?","a":"it bounds (σ + h)ⁿ in one line, so root-of-two’s argument runs for every n","w":["it computes the root to any number of digits","it proves the root is a fraction","it replaces no-holes"],"why":"(σ + h)ⁿ ≤ σⁿ + h((σ + 1)ⁿ − σⁿ) for 0 < h ≤ 1. So every positive real has one positive n-th root."},
 {"u":"8f5.1","q":"Why is √4 not “2 or −2”?","a":"√ names the one non-negative root; it is the equation x² = 4 that has two answers","w":["−2 squared is not 4","√4 has no value","a root is always a fraction"],"why":"And σ² < 2 gives (σ + h)² < 2 only for h small enough: with σ = 1.4 and h = 1, (2.4)² = 5.76."},
 {"u":"8f6.1","q":"You want to prove two expressions with roots are the same. What do you show?","a":"both are non-negative and have the same square, or n-th power","w":["both are fractions","they agree to six decimal places","both are positive and one is at most the other"],"why":"One-root. And wanting a real with a property of powers, take the least upper bound of what falls short, then refute too small and too big with a step 1/n."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["8g"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["8g"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"8g1.1","q":"Which reading of α < β is the one for hands?","a":"β − α is positive","w":["α is a part of β and not the same","α is to the left of β in the picture","α has fewer members than β"],"why":"Unpack < into “something is positive”, use positive-sum or positive-product, pack it back. That rhythm carries every technique of the step."},
 {"u":"8g2.1","q":"p < q and s < 0. What does negative-reverses-order give?","a":"qs < ps","w":["ps < qs","ps = qs","p + s < q"],"why":"q − p and −s are positive, so their product ps − qs is positive. Sign-of-a-product: with no factor 0, a product is positive exactly when an even number of factors are negative."},
 {"u":"8g3.1","q":"In (c, ∞), why does ∞ always take a round bracket?","a":"∞ is not a number: it only says “no end on this side”, so there is no end to keep","w":["∞ is the greatest real","∞ is a member that is left out","round brackets are used for large numbers"],"why":"An interval is a stretch of the line named by its ends. A square bracket keeps an end; there is none to keep at ∞."},
 {"u":"8g4.1","q":"Solving 5 − 3x < 11, what is the one difference from an equation?","a":"multiplying by a negative turns the order about: x > −2","w":["nothing may be added to both sides","the answer is one number","both sides must be squared first"],"why":"Check with a number on each side: x = 0 gives 5 < 11, true; x = −3 gives 14 < 11, false. That catches a forgotten turn."},
 {"u":"8g5.1","q":"Why does a sign chart work?","a":"between the places where a factor is 0 every factor keeps its sign, so count the negatives in each stretch","w":["a product is positive whenever its first factor is","factors change sign at every whole number","the product has the sign of its largest factor"],"why":"Sign-of-a-product reads each stretch. A squared factor never changes sign, so the sign does not flip at its zero."},
 {"u":"8g6.1","q":"(x − 1)/(x + 2) ≥ 0. Why is multiplying both sides by x + 2 a trap?","a":"its sign is unknown: for x < −2 it is negative and the order should have turned","w":["x + 2 is never 0","multiplying is never allowed in an inequality","it makes the top 0"],"why":"It loses x = −3, where the fraction is 4. Honest roads: a sign chart, or multiply by (x + 2)², positive for x ≠ −2."},
 {"u":"8g7.1","q":"x + 1/x ≥ 2 for x > 0 is proved by reaching (x − 1)² ≥ 0. What makes the column honest?","a":"every arrow goes both ways, and the last line is true by square-never-negative","w":["it was checked for x = 1, 2 and 3","the first line was assumed","x is treated as a fraction"],"why":"Multiplying by x is legal because x is given positive, and it is undone by multiplying by 1/x."},
 {"u":"8g8.1","q":"“x² < 3x, so x < 3.” What went wrong?","a":"it divided by x, whose sign is unknown; honestly x(x − 3) < 0, so (0, 3)","w":["nothing: the answer is (−∞, 3)","x² is never below 3x","it should have squared both sides"],"why":"(−∞, 3) wrongly holds −1, where 1 < −3 fails. Likewise same-direction inequalities add, they do not subtract: 1 − 0 < 5 − 4 is false."},
 {"u":"8g9.1","q":"You want an inequality whose solution set is (c, d). What do you write?","a":"(x − c)(x − d) < 0","w":["(x − c)(x − d) > 0","(x − c)² > 0","x − c < d"],"why":"The outside of the interval is the same product > 0. For a turn in the solution, put a negative coefficient on x."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["8h"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["8h"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"8h1.1","q":"|p| is p if 0 ≤ p, and −p if p < 0. Why is it never negative?","a":"in the second case it is the negative of a negative, which is positive","w":["the bars delete the sign as a mark","it is defined only for positive p","it is the square of p"],"why":"So |p| ≥ 0 always, and |p| = 0 only when p = 0. The distance between p and q is |p − q|."},
 {"u":"8h2.1","q":"For d ≥ 0, what does unpack-the-bars turn |p| < d into?","a":"−d < p < d","w":["p < d","p < −d or p > d","−d < p"],"why":"Each technique about bars is proved by splitting on the signs inside the bars with three-way-order, then using the twelve techniques."},
 {"u":"8h3.1","q":"|p + q| ≤ |p| + |q|. When are the two sides equal?","a":"exactly when p and q are not of opposite signs","w":["always","only when p = q","only when both are 0"],"why":"The shorter road adds the two sandwiches −|p| ≤ p ≤ |p| and −|q| ≤ q ≤ |q| part by part, then packs with unpack-the-bars."},
 {"u":"8h4.1","q":"What set is |x − m| < d?","a":"the interval (m − d, m + d): the stretch within d of m","w":["the interval (−d, d)","everything outside (m − d, m + d)","the two reals m − d and m + d"],"why":"Run backwards, (c, e) is |x − (c + e)/2| < (e − c)/2: a middle and a reach on each side."},
 {"u":"8h5.1","q":"Why does |x + 1| > 2 stay split in two?","a":"it is an outside: x + 1 > 2 or x + 1 < −2, two pieces that no double inequality joins","w":["because the bars are never unpacked","because it has no answers","because 2 is positive"],"why":"The method is always to unpack the bars into cases or a double inequality, then use 8g’s techniques. Here x > 1 or x < −3."},
 {"u":"8h6.1","q":"x is within 1 of 3. How do you bound how far x² is from 9?","a":"factor the gap as |x − 3| |x + 3|, bound the far factor by 7, keep the one you control","w":["square the bound on x","add 9 to both sides","drop the bars and solve x² = 9"],"why":"|x² − 9| ≤ 7|x − 3|. Run backwards it is useful: to get x² within 7/1000 of 9, keep x within 1/1000 of 3."},
 {"u":"8h7.1","q":"|a + b| = |a| + |b|? Take a = −2, b = 3.","a":"1 against 5: only ≤ is true, by the triangle technique","w":["both sides are 5","both sides are 1","the left side is larger"],"why":"And “|x| < 3, so x < 3” is true but half: x = −10 passes x < 3 and fails |x| < 3. Unpack to −3 < x < 3."},
 {"u":"8h8.1","q":"|kx + b| = c has the answers −2 and 5. What do you find first to rebuild it?","a":"the middle, 3/2, and the reach, 7/2","w":["the product of the answers","the larger answer","k, by guessing"],"why":"Then write |x − 3/2| = 7/2 and scale. An interval is written the same way: |x − middle| < reach."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["8i"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["8i"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"8i1.1","q":"Which two facts fix a root, and so give the method for every technique about radicals?","a":"it is not negative, and its power gives back the original","w":["it is a fraction, and it is positive","it is less than the original, and positive","it has a decimal, and the decimal ends"],"why":"So show both sides are non-negative with the same power: one-root. A root in a bottom is cleared by multiplying by 1 in a useful form, such as the conjugate."},
 {"u":"8i2.1","q":"Why are fractional powers kept for positive bases?","a":"otherwise the techniques clash: (−8)^(1/3) would be −2, yet (−8)^(2/6) read as ((−8)²)^(1/6) is 2","w":["negative reals have no cube roots","fractions cannot stand in the high place","positive bases make the powers whole"],"why":"u^(−k) must be the reciprocal of u^k for u^(−k) u^k = u⁰ to hold, and u^(1/q) must be the q-th root for (u^(1/q))^q = u."},
 {"u":"8i3.1","q":"What does the completed form t(x − h)² + k say?","a":"the value at x = h is k; for t > 0 no value is below k, for t < 0 none is above","w":["the answers are h and k","the value at x = k is h","the expression is never 0"],"why":"Completing the sameness: x² + bx = (x + b/2)² − (b/2)². Its accepted name is completing the square; no shape is meant."},
 {"u":"8i4.1","q":"In deriving the quadratic formula, which move is not licensed both ways?","a":"taking roots, which needs b² − 4tc ≥ 0","w":["dividing by t","adding (b/2t)² to both sides","moving c across"],"why":"The discriminant decides: positive, two answers; 0, one; negative, none on the line, since a square is never negative."},
 {"u":"8i5.1","q":"√(x + 3) = x − 3 is squared and gives x = 1 and x = 6. Why must both be checked?","a":"squaring gains answers: p² = q² gives only |p| = |q|, and x = 1 is a stranger","w":["squaring loses answers","the formula may have been misremembered","6 is too large to be a root"],"why":"At x = 1, √4 = 2 against −2. A quadratic in costume, such as x⁴ − 5x² + 4 = 0, is solved by naming x² with a letter."},
 {"u":"8i6.1","q":"“(x + 3)² = 4, so x + 3 = 2.” What is lost?","a":"x = −5: rooting gives |x + 3| = 2, two cases","w":["nothing","x = −1","x = 2"],"why":"And √(a + b) is not √a + √b: √(9 + 16) = 5, while √9 + √16 = 7. Roots go through products, not sums."},
 {"u":"8i7.1","q":"Handed two answers r and s, which quadratic has them?","a":"x² − (r + s)x + rs = 0","w":["x² + (r + s)x + rs = 0","x² − rs·x + (r + s) = 0","(x + r)(x + s) = 0"],"why":"Sum-and-product. And wanting u^x for a fraction x, take the root first, then the power."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["8·cb5"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["8·cb5"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"8·cb5.1.1","q":"A splitting of ℚ puts every fraction in a lower part or an upper part. How many reals are at it?","a":"exactly one","w":["none, when no fraction is at it","two: one for each part","as many as there are fractions in the lower part"],"why":"Holes-filled. The lower part gives the real, with its greatest member dropped if it has one. A hole is a splitting with no fraction at it, and now a real stands there."},
 {"u":"8·cb5.2.1","q":"p/q is a near miss, p² − 2q² = ±1. How near does it come to √2?","a":"within 1/(2q²), and never all the way, since √2 is no fraction","w":["within 1/q, and no nearer","exactly to √2 for large q","within 1/2, whatever q is"],"why":"|p/q − √2| = 1/(q(p + q√2)) < 1/(2q²). So 7/5 is within 1/50 and 99/70 within 1/9800."},
 {"u":"8·cb5.3.1","q":"What real does an endless decimal name?","a":"the least upper bound of its cut-offs","w":["its last cut-off","the greatest of its cut-offs","a fraction just below its cut-offs"],"why":"So 0.999⋯ = 1: the cut-offs have least upper bound 1. That is no paradox. A decimal is a name, and a real may have two, as 1/2 and 2/4 name one fraction."},
 {"u":"8·cb5.4.1","q":"Dividing v by b place by place, why must the decimal of v/b repeat?","a":"only b remainders are possible, so one comes again, and each digit depends only on the remainder before","w":["because b is prime","because the digits run out","because v is smaller than b"],"why":"Pigeonhole on b + 1 remainders. The other way, a repeating decimal shifted by its block and taken from itself leaves a fraction."},
 {"u":"8·cb5.5.1","q":"“0.999⋯ < 1, since every cut-off is.” What is wrong?","a":"the value is the least upper bound of the cut-offs, which is 1; it need not be a cut-off","w":["some cut-off equals 1","the cut-offs have no upper bound","0.999⋯ names no real"],"why":"8c6 again: a sup that is not a member. What every member has, the sup need not have."},
 {"u":"8·cb5.6.1","q":"Handed a splitting of the fractions, how do you find the real at it?","a":"ask whether the lower part has a greatest member: if so, that fraction; if not, the lower part itself","w":["take the least member of the lower part","take any member of the upper part","average one member of each part"],"why":"A cut has no greatest member, so the one possible greatest member is the fraction at the split."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["8·cb6"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["8·cb6"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"8·cb6.1.1","q":"Equal temperament asks for s with s¹² = 2. Why is s no fraction?","a":"count the 2s: p¹² has a multiple of 12 of them, 2q¹² one more than a multiple of 12","w":["because 12 is not prime","because 2 is even","because s is larger than 1"],"why":"It exists by roots-of-every-order: exactly one positive real has that twelfth power, s = 2^(1/12)."},
 {"u":"8·cb6.2.1","q":"Twelve tempered fifths close on seven octaves exactly. What did each fifth give up?","a":"the twelfth root of the comma 3¹²/2¹⁹, a factor of about 1.00113","w":["the whole comma","one twelfth of an octave","nothing: a tempered fifth is 3/2"],"why":"(3/2)/2^(7/12) to the power 12 is 3¹²/2¹⁹. The comma that pure fifths overshot by is shared out in twelve equal factors."},
 {"u":"8·cb6.3.1","q":"The open strings are 55 · 2^(k/12) Hz for k = −5, 0, 5, 10. Why do strings tuned by harmonics until the beats stop drift from the tuner?","a":"that makes the strings pure fourths apart, and a pure fourth is not five tempered steps","w":["the tuner rounds to whole numbers","harmonics are always flat","the strings stretch as they are tuned"],"why":"The 4th harmonic of E₁ is 164.814 Hz against 165 for the 3rd of A₁: a tempered fourth beats about 0.186 times a second."},
 {"u":"8·cb6.4.1","q":"The fret that raises a note k steps sits at L(1 − 2^(−k/12)) from the nut. Why is the eighteenth-part spacing slightly wrong?","a":"it makes each step 18/17, and twelve of them stack to about 1.9856, short of 2","w":["it puts fret 12 past the middle of the string","it uses 2^(1/12) exactly","it ignores the scale length"],"why":"The exact fraction is one 17.817th of the remaining length. With eighteen, the twelfth fret lands about 3 mm near and its octave sounds flat."},
 {"u":"8·cb6.5.1","q":"A tempered fifth beats: the 3rd harmonic of the lower note misses the 2nd of the upper. Why does the beat rate grow with pitch?","a":"the two harmonics differ by a fixed ratio, so their difference in Hz scales with the frequency","w":["higher notes are louder","the fifth gets wider as pitch rises","the tuner is less exact up there"],"why":"On A₁ the fifth beats about 0.186 times a second, on A₃ about 0.745: four times the frequency, four times the beats."},
 {"u":"8·cb6.6.1","q":"“Twelve steps make an octave, so one step is the ratio 1 + 1/12.” What is wrong?","a":"ratios stack by multiplying: twelve steps of 13/12 give about 2.61, not 2","w":["twelve steps of 13/12 give exactly 2","a step is 1/7 of an octave","ratios stack by adding"],"why":"The step is the twelfth root of 2, about 1.05946."},
 {"u":"8·cb6.7.1","q":"Handed a fret’s distance from the nut, how do you find which step it is?","a":"divide by the scale length and solve 1 − 2^(−k/12) for k by trying whole k","w":["divide the distance by 12","count the frets below it by eye","multiply by the frequency"],"why":"Found upward; written downward. The technique that placed the fret, run the other way."});
